<title>PHILIPPINES: PHILIPPINE STOCKS - Favourites for 1997.</title>
<headline>PHILIPPINE STOCKS - Favourites for 1997.</headline>
<dateline>MANILA 1996-12-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>Following are the five favourite blue-chips on the Philippine stock exchange for 1997, according to a poll of five Manila brokers.</p>
<p>The respondents also picked their five favourite secondary stocks for the year, a list of which is attached.</p>
<p>	 Stock				   Comments</p>
<p>SAN MIGUEL CORP		   Picked by all five respondents.</p>
<p>					It was called a premier consumer</p>
<p>					play and seen to benefit from low</p>
<p>					inflation and higher revenue</p>
<p>					contribution from foreign</p>
<p>					investments.</p>
<p>MANILA ELECRIC CO (MERALCO)   Picked by three of five</p>
<p>					respondents.</p>
<p>					One said it had the most reliable</p>
<p>					earnings potential among all</p>
<p>					blue-chips and another said strong</p>
<p>					GDP growth should ensure healthy</p>
<p>					sales.</p>
<p>SM PRIME HOLDINGS INC (SMPH)  Mall operator SM Prime was chosen</p>
<p>					by three of five brokers. One</p>
<p>					research head said the stock is</p>
<p>					undervalued and is expected to</p>
<p>					pick</p>
<p>					up on increased consumer spending.</p>
<p>PETRON CORP			 Oil refiner Petron, partly owned</p>
<p>					by Saudi Aramco, gained two votes</p>
<p>					from the five respondents. The company 					is seen to benefit from full</p>
<p>					deregulation of the oil industry</p>
<p>					in March 1997. Another broker said</p>
<p>					payment by government of the</p>
<p>					company's over one billion peso</p>
<p>					claim from the Oil Price</p>
<p>					Stabilisation Fund will boost the</p>
<p>					issue.</p>
<p>PHILIPPINE LONG DISTANCE	The country's dominant carrier</p>
<p>TELEPHONE CO (PLDT)	     was chosen by two of the five</p>
<p>					brokerage houses. With the</p>
<p>					company's Zero Backlog Project</p>
<p>					onstream and the prospects of</p>
<p>					implementing a metering scheme</p>
<p>					next year, analysts said PLDT</p>
<p>					is still a favoured stock.</p>
<p>AYALA LAND B (ALI)		Ayala Land, the real estate unit</p>
<p>					of diversified firm Ayala Corp,</p>
<p>					emerged as the choice of two of 					the five brokers. Despite perceptions</p>
<p>					of 					a glut in the high-end real estate</p>
<p>					market, Ayala Land will still be</p>
<p>					the dominant player, they said.</p>
<p>			    ====================</p>
<p>Among the small caps, MUSIC Semiconductors Inc, the only high-technology stock listed at the Philippine Stock Exchange, garnered two votes while the rest of the issues got one vote each.</p>
<p>The secondary and tertiary issues picked by the polled brokers were the following:</p>
<p>ANG PING SECURITIES	    MUSIC Semiconductors Inc,</p>
<p>				     Holding firm Crown Equities, 				     gaming firm Mondragon</p>
<p>				     International Philippines Inc, 				     Kuok Philippines</p>
<p>				     Properties Inc, and Lorenzo</p>
<p>				     Shipping Corp</p>
<p>BPI CAPITAL			Fastfood chain Jollibee Food Corp, 				     ABS-CBN Brodcasting Co, 				     liquor firm La Tondena</p>
<p>				     Distillers Inc, gaming and</p>
<p>				     real estate firm Belle Corporation</p>
<p>CITISECURITIES		   Cosmos Bottling Corp,</p>
<p>				     International Container Terminal</p>
<p>				     Services Inc, Music</p>
<p>				     Semiconductors, Universal</p>
<p>Rightfield</p>
<p>				     Property and construction</p>
<p>				     giant DMCI Holdings Inc</p>
<p>DHARMALA SECURITIES	    Local Sony Corp and Aiwa products</p>
<p>				     distributor Solid Group In, 				     Engineering Equipment Inc, 				     corrugated box maker</p>
<p>				     Steniel Manufacturing,</p>
<p>				     Empire East Land Inc, and</p>
<p>				     Fortune Cement Corp</p>
<p>PRYCE SECURITIES		 Republic Glass Holdings,</p>
<p>				     retail chain and property developer</p>
<p>				     Uniwide Holdings Inc, and</p>
<p>				     Asiatrust Development Bank Inc</p>
<p>The brokerage houses and analysts/research directors surveyed were:</p>
<p>Ang Ping Securities Inc</p>
<p>Wilfred Son Keng Po, chief operations officer</p>
<p>BPI Capital Corp</p>
<p>Spencer Yap, research head</p>
<p>Citisecurities Inc</p>
<p>Cilette Liboro, research head</p>
<p>Dharmala Securities (Phils) Inc</p>
<p>Fitz Aclan, research head</p>
<p>Pryce Securities Inc</p>
<p>Jonas Ferrer, research director</p>
